"Large-scale plasma proteomics comparisons through genetics and disease associations | Nature (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06563-x)\nciteturn39view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn36view2\",\"pattern\":\"epitope\"}); Redirected to URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06563-x?error=cookies_not_supported&code=f57098a4-c007-4ecc-a887-ec42c028bb46; Total lines: 991\nL165: Secondary cis pQTL associations were detected for 1,702 out of 2,102 sentinel cis pQTLs on the Olink platform (81%) and for 1,594 out of 2,120 on the SomaScan platform (75%). Secondary trans pQTL associations were detected for 3,340 out of 24,824 sentinel trans pQTL associations on the Olink platform (13%) and 4,065 out of 22,616 on the SomaScan platform (18%).\nL166: Whereas secondary signals help to understand how genetic variation affects protein expression, the mere existence of a cis pQTL for a protein on a particular platform provides evidence that the assay is binding to the correct protein, even though the pQTL may in fact be the result of an epitope effect (that is, the genetic variant directly affects binding of the antibody to its epitope) and not reflect actual variation in protein levels.\nL167: On the Olink platform, the majority of proteins already have a cis pQTL. Furthermore, the significance of most cis pQTLs is well above the genome-wide threshold, suggesting that the number of cis pQTLs is unlikely to change drastically with increased sample size, although cis pQTLs still provide valuable insights into the genetic control of protein expression.\nL168: However, as most trans pQTL associations have significance close to the genome-wide threshold, expanding the sample size is likely to reveal more trans pQTLs (Extended Data Fig. cite119†5 ).\nL169: ## pQTL analysis by ancestry group\nL170: Analyses of different ancestry groups enables the assessment of greater sequence diversity and variable patterns of LD to refine association signals to fewer variants^{cite96†12 }. Recently, Katz and colleagues used the Olink Explore 1536 platform to analyse the levels of 1,472 proteins in the plasma of 489 individuals with African ancestries^{cite93†10 }.\nL171: Applying the same cut-off for significance (P < 1.8 × 10^{−9}) to both datasets, for the same 1,472 proteins we detected cis pQTLs in the UKB-AF dataset (n = 1,513 individuals) for 628 proteins, whereas they detected only 307 using a 3 times smaller sample size. We validated the existence of cis pQTLs for 301 of their 307 proteins.\nL194: The large number of whole-genome-sequenced individuals in the two study populations, followed by imputation, enabled us to detect associations with rare sequence variants (Table cite116†1 ). On Olink, 2,505 variants with a MAF below 0.1% (10% of 25,147 variants) associated with protein levels, and 1,596 variants (8% of 19,225 variants) did so on SomaScan.\nL195: ## Relationship of pQTLs between platforms\nL196: The fraction of sentinel cis pQTLs in high LD (r^{2} > 0.80) with protein-altering variants (PAVs) (33% for both Olink and SomaScan) or cis expression quantitative trait loci (eQTLs) (39% and 35% for Olink and SomaScan, respectively), for the gene encoding the targeted protein, was similar on the two platforms (Supplementary Tables cite98†21 and cite98†19 ).\nL197: We previously concluded that the presence of a PAV and the absence of eQTL could be evidence of the association resulting from an epitope effect^{cite77†2 } (Supplementary Note cite99†11 ). We observe very similar results on both platforms. We conclude that the 23% of sentinel Olink cis pQTLs and 24% of sentinel SomaScan cis pQTLs that are in high LD with a moderate-effect PAV but not with a cis eQTL are likely to be caused by epitope effects and may not in fact reflect variation in protein levels (Fig.\nL198: cite131†3 and Extended Data Fig. cite119†5 ). When attempting to replicate sentinel cis pQTLs between platforms, we observed that when cis pQTLs are in high LD with PAV, the correlations between effect estimates were lower in both directions of replication, suggesting that both Olink and SomaScan assays are similarly susceptible to epitope effects caused by PAVs (Extended Data Fig. cite132†7 , Supplementary Note cite99†12 , Supplementary Fig. cite99†7 and Supplementary Tables cite98†30 –cite98†34 ).\nL199: Fig. 3: Effect of alternative alleles.\nL200: \nL201: cite133†Image: Fig. 3: Effect of alternative alleles.†media.springernature.com L202: \nL203: cite134†Full size image L204: Effect on each platform of alternative alleles broken down by the presence or absence of PAVs or cis eQTLs in high LD. PAV-M, moderate-impact PAV; PAV-H, high-impact PAV; n_{O}, number of cis pQTLs detected with Olink; n_{S}, number of cis pQTLs detected with SomaScan. Box plots show the median and lower and upper quartiles; whiskers extend to 1.5 times the interquartile range; points beyond whiskers are plotted individually.\nL205: Although replication of pQTLs between platforms was similar to previous reports in smaller studies^{cite77†2 ,cite91†3 ,cite88†6 ,cite89†7 } (Supplementary Note cite99†12 ), the presence of multiple independent signals at the same locus makes the comparison of pQTLs complicated, as the sentinel signal in one cohort may be a secondary signal in the other.\nL206: To establish correspondence between pQTLs on the Olink and SomaScan platforms, we checked whether the sentinel variant detected on one platform was in high LD (r^{2} > 0.8) with any of the pQTLs (sentinel or secondary) at the same locus (within 5 Mb) on the other platform. In the UKB-BI Olink data, 581 (40%) out of 1,468 sentinel cis pQTL signals had a corresponding pQTL in the Icelandic SomaScan data, and in 434 cases (30%) the pQTL was the sentinel signal at the locus.\nL270: Furthermore, some cis pQTLs may correspond to epitope effects rather than protein levels, particularly when the cis pQTL correlates with a coding variant and in the absence of a correlated eQTL^{cite77†2 ,cite89†7 }. The differences that we note in plasma protein levels or in the pQTLs between platforms influence the results from integrating protein levels and genetics in the study of diseases.\nL282: The platforms may be differently affected by epitope effects. We have not systematically performed assays such as ELISA or used other methods for each of the proteins or proteoforms studied, as such validation is currently difficult to perform at scale. Where the results from the platforms are discordant, further studies are required to determine which platform to believe, although evidence such as orthogonal validation or the existence of cis pQTLs can provide some insight.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLarge-scale plasma proteomics comparisons through genetics and disease associations | Nature (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06563-x)\nciteturn39view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn36view2\",\"pattern\":\"IL6R\"}); Redirected to URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06563-x?error=cookies_not_supported&code=f57098a4-c007-4ecc-a887-ec42c028bb46; Total lines: 991\nNo matching text found for \"IL6R\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://rnajc.ucsf.edu/sites/rnajc.ucsf.edu/files/nbt.3973_0.pdf)\nciteturn39view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 7; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn36view3\",\"pattern\":\"CD4\"}); Total lines: 942\nL27@P0: Protein and mRNA expression of canonical markers for monocytes\nL28@P0: (CD11b, CD14, CD33), B cells (CD20, CD19), T cells (CD3, CD4, CD8),\nL29@P0: and natural killer (NK) cells (CD56, CD158e1) were projected on the\nL30@P0: mRNA t-SNE plot to visualize expression across all PBMCs, and to assess\nL31@P0: the specificity and sensitivity of the protein and mRNA assays (Fig. 1b).\nL32@P0: For each marker, the Pearson correlation coefficient between mRNA and\nL33@P0: protein expression was calculated. The markers most highly correlated\nL34@P0: were HLA-DR (R = 0.69), CD20 (R = 0.46), and CD14 (R = 0.51), and\nL35@P0: these markers also had the highest levels of transcriptional expression\nL36@P0: (Supplementary Table 3). For CD4, the correlation between mRNA and\nL37@P0: protein was low, and we found it expressed both in monocytes and T\nL38@P0: cells, a finding we confirmed by flow cytometry, ruling out non-specific\nL39@P0: Multiplexed quantification\nL40@P0: of proteins and transcripts\nL41@P0: in single cells\nL42@P0: Vanessa M Peterson1,5, Kelvin Xi Zhang2,5,\nL80@P0-1: BRIEF COMMUNICATIONS © 2017 Nature America, Inc., part of Springer Nature. All rights reserved.NATURE BIOTECHNOLOGY VOLUME 35 NUMBER 10 OCTOBER 2017 937\nL81@P1: binding of the CD4 AbB (Fig. 1c). Markers such as CD56, CD158e1,\nL82@P1: CD19, CD33, CD11b, and CD155 all showed higher sensitivity at the\nL83@P1: protein level than mRNA. However, measuring the entire transcriptome\nL84@P1: enabled us to identify plasmacytoid dendritic cells (LILRA4, SERPINF1),\nL85@P1: megakaryoctyes (PF4), and FCGR3A+ monocytes (FCGR3A), as these\nL86@P1: markers were not included in our AbB panel (Fig. 1d).\nL87@P1: We compared REAP-seq protein measurements to flow cytometry and\nL88@P1: found agreement in the relative abundances of the four major cell types\nL89@P1: identified (Fig. 1e). REAP-seq protein measurements of CD45RO and\nL90@P1: CD45RA (alternatively spliced isoforms of CD45) were also consistent\nL91@P1: with flow cytometry data showing high CD45RA expression in CD20+ B\nL92@P1: cells and high CD45RO expression in CD14+ monocytes (Fig. 1f). These\nL93@P1: isoforms are not measured by 3’ mRNA sequencing, demonstrating the\nL94@P1: utility of this method to measure proteins and transcripts in a single assay.\nL95@P1: Agonist monoclonal antibodies to CD27 (aCD27) have been shown\nL96@P1: to be useful in effectively modulating immune responses includ\u0002ing antitumor immunity in preclinical models14,15. We used REAP\u0002seq to characterize the in vitro activation of naive CD8+ T cells with\nL97@P1: Figure 1 Benchmarking of REAP-seq on PBMCs. (a) PBMCs (n = 7,271) were processed with REAP-seq. t-SNE visualization of eight clusters identified\nL98@P1: using the top ten significant principal components across 1,664 variable genes. Cells are colored by cluster. (b) mRNA and protein signal for canonical\nL99@P1: markers expressed in monocytes (CD11b, CD33, CD14, CD155), B cells (CD19, CD20), T cells (CD3, CD4, CD8), and NK cells (CD56, CD158e1) were\nL100@P1: projected on the t-SNE plot from a. For each marker, the Pearson correlation coefficient (R) between mRNA and protein expression across 7,271 single cells\nL101@P1: is displayed. (c) Flow cytometry staining confirms that CD14+ monocytes have low level of CD4+ expression, which was seen in the REAP-seq t-SNE plot\nL102@P1: showing CD4 protein expression (b, upper left cluster in CD4 protein panel). (d) mRNA signal for markers expressed in plasmacytoid dendritic cells (pDCs;\nL103@P1: LILRA4, SERPINF1), FCGR3A+ monocytes (FCGR3A), and megakaryocytes (PF4) were projected on the t-SNE plot from a. (e) REAP-seq conducted on\nL104@P1: 7,271 PBMCs compared to flow cytometry on 290,750 PBMCs (from the same sample). REAP-seq protein counts were log normalized, first scaling each cell\nL105@P1: to a total of 1 × 104 molecules and then analyzed in Cytobank along with flow cytometry data. (f) REAP-seq protein assay enables measurement of CD45RO\nL106@P1: and CD45RA isoforms that cannot be measured with 3' RNA-seq methods. Flow cytometry data confirmed the high expression of CD45RA in CD20+ cells and\nL107@P1: CD45RO in CD14+ cells. The color of each dot in the bivariate plot represents th